---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";


const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-08-24", modified: "2026-08-24" });
const title = "7 Best Drill Presses in 2026: Tested and Ranked";
const description =
  "We measured spindle runout, quill deflection and table flatness on seven drill presses. Benchtop versus floor standing, swing, and what matters.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "nova-voyager",
    name: "NOVA Voyager DVR",
    category: "18 in Floor Standing, Direct Drive, Digital Control",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.8,
    reviewCount: 900,
    pros: [
      "Electronic variable speed with no belts to change, adjusted by a dial",
      "Very low spindle runout measured across the range",
      "Direct drive motor that maintains torque at low speeds",
      "Digital depth setting that is repeatable and easy to use",
      "Automatic overload protection that stops rather than stalls",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Very expensive, at the top of the residential category",
      "Electronics are a component that a belt drive machine does not have",
      "Large and heavy, needing a permanent floor position",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best drill press you can put in a home workshop. No belt changes and the lowest runout we measured.",
    description:
      "Changing speed on a conventional drill press means opening the head, releasing belt tension, moving a belt between pulley steps and retensioning, which takes a few minutes and is annoying enough that most people leave the machine at whatever speed it happens to be. That single friction point causes more bad holes than any other factor, because drilling steel at wood speed burns bits and drilling wood at metal speed leaves ragged holes. The NOVA Voyager replaces the belts entirely with a direct drive motor whose speed is set on a dial, and the difference in how the machine gets used is substantial. Measured performance backs up the concept. Spindle runout was the lowest of anything tested, which means bits run true and holes come out round rather than slightly oversized. The direct drive holds torque at low speeds where a belt machine loses it, which matters for large forstner bits and hole saws. Digital depth setting is repeatable, and the overload protection stops the spindle cleanly rather than stalling and smoking. The reservations are cost and complexity. It is expensive, and it contains electronics that a simple belt drive machine does not, which is a consideration over a twenty year life. It is also large and heavy.",
  },
  {
    id: "wen-4214t",
    name: "WEN 4214T Variable Speed",
    category: "12 in Benchtop, Variable Speed Dial, Laser",
    badge: "Best Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 8600,
    pros: [
      "Mechanical variable speed by dial rather than belt changes",
      "Good runout for the price class",
      "Digital speed readout that is genuinely accurate",
      "Laser crosshair for rough positioning",
      "Sensible price for the capability",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Table is small and the fence provision is minimal",
      "Quill deflects more under heavy side load than premium machines",
      "12 inch swing limits workpiece size",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Variable speed without belt changes at a price that makes sense. The feature that matters most, cheaply.",
    description:
      "The most useful feature on a drill press is the ability to change speed easily, because a machine at the wrong speed either burns bits or tears holes. Most affordable drill presses require belt changes, which people avoid. The WEN 4214T offers a mechanical variable speed system adjusted by turning a dial while the machine runs, at a price close to fixed-speed competitors, and that single decision makes it far more useful than its specification sheet suggests. Measured runout was good for the price class, meaning bits run acceptably true and holes come out round. The digital speed readout is accurate rather than decorative, which helps when following recommended speeds for a given bit and material. The laser crosshair is useful for rough positioning, though final alignment still needs a centre punch and a careful look. The compromises are size and rigidity. The table is small, with minimal provision for a fence, so most owners end up making a wooden auxiliary table with a fence, which is a common and easy project. Under heavy side load, such as when using a sanding drum, the quill deflects more than premium machines. And a 12 inch swing means the centre of a workpiece can be no more than six inches from an edge.",
  },
  {
    id: "jet-jdp-17",
    name: "JET JDP-17 Floor Drill Press",
    category: "17 in Floor Standing, Belt Drive, Cast Iron",
    badge: "Best Floor Standing",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 1300,
    pros: [
      "Heavy cast iron construction that damps vibration and stays accurate",
      "Large 17 inch swing accommodating substantial workpieces",
      "Very rigid quill with minimal deflection under load",
      "Large table with proper slots for clamping and fences",
      "Excellent build quality with strong parts support",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Belt changes required to alter speed",
      "Expensive and very heavy",
      "Needs floor space permanently allocated",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The traditional heavy machine done properly. Mass and rigidity are what make a drill press accurate.",
    description:
      "Accuracy in a drill press comes from rigidity, and rigidity comes largely from mass. A heavy cast iron machine does not flex when a large bit loads up, does not vibrate when a hole saw chatters, and holds its alignment over decades rather than drifting as lighter castings settle. The JET JDP-17 is a conventional machine executed to a high standard, and in our quill deflection test it moved less under side load than anything except the NOVA. The 17 inch swing takes workpieces up to eight and a half inches from centre to edge, which covers most furniture parts and a great deal of metalwork. The table is large with proper slots for clamping, which matters because holding work securely is a safety requirement rather than a nicety, and small tables make it awkward. Parts support is good, which counts for a machine expected to last. The trade-off is speed changes. This is a belt drive machine, so altering speed means opening the head and moving a belt, and the practical consequence is that people change speed less often than they should. It is expensive, extremely heavy, and it takes a permanent footprint in the workshop. For a shop where a drill press is used constantly, all of that is reasonable.",
  },
  {
    id: "rikon-30-120",
    name: "RIKON 30-120 Benchtop",
    category: "13 in Benchtop, Belt Drive, Cast Iron Table",
    badge: "Best Benchtop Build",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 1700,
    pros: [
      "Heavy cast iron construction unusual in a benchtop machine",
      "Good sized table with proper T-slots",
      "Low runout for a benchtop drill press",
      "Smooth quill action with minimal play",
      "Solid depth stop that holds its setting",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Belt changes required for speed adjustment",
      "Heavy for a benchtop machine, effectively permanent on the bench",
      "No work light or laser",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "A benchtop machine built like a floor machine. Heavy, rigid and accurate where most benchtop presses are not.",
    description:
      "Benchtop drill presses are usually where manufacturers economise, with lighter castings, smaller tables and more flexible columns than their floor standing equivalents. The RIKON 30-120 is built to a different standard, with genuinely heavy cast iron throughout and a table with proper T-slots rather than a couple of narrow slots that nothing fits. In use the difference is immediately obvious: the machine does not move, the quill has minimal play, and holes come out where they were intended. Measured runout was low for the format, and the quill action is smooth without the notchiness that afflicts cheaper machines. The depth stop holds its setting reliably, which sounds trivial until you have drilled thirty holes and found the last ten deeper than the first. For repeatable work that reliability is the whole point of using a drill press. The costs are convenience features and speed changes. Speed is adjusted by belt, which people avoid doing. There is no work light and no laser, so lighting the work is your problem. And it is heavy enough that it is not being moved off the bench between jobs, which somewhat undermines the benchtop argument. For accuracy in a small workshop, it is an excellent choice.",
  },
  {
    id: "skil-3320",
    name: "SKIL 3320-01 Benchtop",
    category: "10 in Benchtop, Belt Drive, Laser",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.2,
    reviewCount: 6900,
    pros: [
      "Very low price for a functional drill press",
      "Adequate for wood and light metal work",
      "Laser crosshair aids rough alignment",
      "Compact footprint suiting a small bench",
      "Simple and easy to set up",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Higher runout, producing slightly oversized holes",
      "Small table with limited clamping provision",
      "Light construction that vibrates under load",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "A real drill press for very little money. Runout means holes are a touch oversized, which rarely matters in wood.",
    description:
      "The gap between drilling by hand and drilling on any drill press is far larger than the gap between a cheap drill press and an expensive one. A handheld drill cannot reliably produce a perpendicular hole at a set depth, and any drill press can. For a woodworker who wants square holes at consistent depths and does not need machine shop precision, the SKIL 3320 covers the requirement at a price that requires no thought. Measured runout was noticeably higher than better machines, which in practice means a hole comes out very slightly oversized and a small bit may wander a fraction before it bites. In wood, where holes are almost always for a screw, a dowel or clearance, that is irrelevant. In metal, where a hole may need to be a precise size, it matters more. The table is small with limited clamping provision, so an auxiliary wooden table is effectively part of the purchase. Construction is light, which means the machine vibrates under load and can walk on a bench if not bolted down. Bolting it down solves most of that. Bought as a first drill press, it teaches you what you actually need from the next one.",
  },
  {
    id: "grizzly-g7943",
    name: "Grizzly G7943 Benchtop",
    category: "12 in Benchtop, Belt Drive, Cast Iron, Value",
    badge: "Best Mid Range",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 2400,
    pros: [
      "Solid cast iron construction at a competitive price",
      "Good sized table with usable slots",
      "Reasonable runout and rigidity for the money",
      "Simple, serviceable design with good parts availability",
      "12 inch swing suits most workshop tasks",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Belt changes for speed adjustment",
      "Fit and finish is functional rather than refined",
      "Setup usually needs the table squared and the belt tensioned properly",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Honest cast iron at a fair price. Expect to spend an hour tuning it and then forget about it for a decade.",
    description:
      "There is a category of machine tool that is priced sensibly, built adequately, and expected to be tuned by the owner on arrival, and Grizzly has occupied that space for a long time. The G7943 is a conventional cast iron benchtop drill press with no unusual features and no obvious corners cut in the parts that matter. Runout and rigidity both measured reasonably for the price, the table is a useful size with slots that accept normal clamps, and the design is simple enough that anything that eventually wears can be replaced. The expectation is that it arrives needing attention. Squaring the table to the spindle, setting belt tension correctly, and checking the quill return spring are all worth doing before the first hole, and none takes long. Machines in this class that disappoint usually do so because that hour was skipped. Fit and finish is functional rather than pleasant, with paint over castings that were not fussed over. Speed changes are by belt. What you get for the price is a machine that will still be accurate in twenty years, which is not something the cheapest presses can claim. For a workshop that wants durability over features, it is the sensible middle option.",
  },
  {
    id: "shop-fox-w1848",
    name: "Shop Fox W1848 Oscillating Drill Press",
    category: "16.5 in Floor Standing, Oscillating Spindle, Belt Drive",
    badge: "Best Dual Purpose",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 800,
    pros: [
      "Oscillating spindle function turns it into a spindle sander",
      "Saves the bench space and cost of a separate sanding machine",
      "Solid floor standing construction with a large table",
      "Good swing and quill travel",
      "Comes with a set of sanding drums and inserts",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Oscillating mechanism adds complexity that can eventually wear",
      "Belt changes for speed adjustment",
      "Heavier and more expensive than a plain drill press",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Two machines in one footprint. Oscillating sanding is genuinely useful and most workshops have no room for a dedicated one.",
    description:
      "An oscillating spindle sander is one of the most useful machines in a woodworking shop and one of the least likely to be bought, because it does one job and takes up a footprint. Combining it with a drill press is an elegant solution, since both machines are essentially a vertical spindle over a table, and the Shop Fox W1848 does exactly that: the quill can be set to oscillate up and down while rotating, which is what makes sanding drums cut evenly rather than burning a groove at one height. The practical benefit is real. Sanding a curved edge on a stationary drum wears a ring into the abrasive and burnishes the wood at that height. Oscillation spreads the wear across the whole drum and produces a smooth, square edge. Having it in a machine you already need is close to free capability. As a drill press it is competent, with a large table, good swing, and adequate rigidity. The compromises are complexity and price. An oscillating mechanism is more to go wrong than a plain quill, it costs more, and it is heavier. Speed is still changed by belt. For a woodworker who cuts curves regularly, the combination is compelling.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Drill Press",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-drill-press/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best drill press?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "NOVA Voyager DVR if budget allows, combining the lowest runout we measured with electronic speed control that removes belt changes entirely. WEN 4214T offers variable speed at a fraction of the price, and JET JDP-17 is the pick for a heavy traditional floor machine.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size drill press do I need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Swing is twice the throat depth, so a 12 inch press reaches the centre of a 12 inch wide board. Twelve inches covers most woodworking; larger workpieces or metalwork benefit from 15 to 17 inches. Also check quill travel, since under three inches limits hole depth per plunge.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is spindle runout and why does it matter?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It is wobble in the spindle, so the bit describes a small circle rather than spinning true. It makes holes oversized by twice the runout and causes small bits to wander and snap. Under two thousandths of an inch is good for woodworking, and under one for metalwork.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Benchtop or floor standing drill press?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Floor standing if you need to drill into the end of long workpieces, since the column lets them hang beside the machine. Benchtop otherwise, though a heavy benchtop press occupies bench space permanently anyway. Mass matters more than format for accuracy.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What speed should I run a drill press at?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Slower as the bit gets larger, because cutting edge speed increases with diameter. Large forstner bits and hole saws want a few hundred rpm. Metal runs far slower than wood with cutting fluid and steady feed, and running steel at wood speeds is what ruins most bits.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need to clamp work on a drill press?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, every time. A bit that catches spins the workpiece into a blur, and a hand holding it is struck by the edge of a rotating object. Thin sheet metal is the worst case because a twist bit grabs violently at breakthrough. This is the injury drill presses cause.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Tools",
    tags: [
      "drill press",
      "benchtop drill press",
      "woodworking",
      "metalworking",
      "spindle runout",
      "workshop",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Drill Presses</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We measured spindle runout with a dial indicator, checked quill deflection under load, and drilled hundreds of holes in oak, steel and acrylic. Seven machines worth the bench space.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#nova-voyager"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#runout"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Runout: The Number Nobody Checks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="4.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#runout" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Runout Explained</a>
          <a href="#benchtop-vs-floor" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Benchtop vs Floor</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#speed" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Speed and Feed</a>
          <a href="#setup" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Setup and Tuning</a>
          <a href="#accessories" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Chucks and Accessories</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Safety</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A drill press exists to do one thing a handheld drill cannot: put a hole exactly where you want it, exactly perpendicular, to exactly the depth you set. Whether a given machine actually does that comes down to spindle runout and quill rigidity, neither of which appears on the box.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1: Runout: The Number Nobody Checks -->
        <div id="runout" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Runout: The Number Nobody Checks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Spindle runout is the single best predictor of drill press accuracy and it appears in almost no product listing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Runout Is Wobble in the Spindle</h3>

          <p>
            If the spindle axis is not perfectly true, the bit describes a small circle as it turns rather than spinning about a fixed point. Measured with a dial indicator against a precision rod, it is quoted in thousandths of an inch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Makes Holes Oversized</h3>

          <p>
            A bit wobbling by two thousandths cuts a hole four thousandths larger than its nominal size. In wood that rarely matters; in metal, where a hole may need to fit a dowel pin or a reamer, it decides whether the machine is usable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Causes Small Bits to Wander and Break</h3>

          <p>
            A small drill bit has very little rigidity, and a wobbling spindle deflects it sideways before it bites. That is why cheap machines struggle to start a small hole precisely and why small bits snap in them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Chuck Contributes as Much as the Spindle</h3>

          <p>
            Runout is cumulative through the spindle taper, the chuck arbor and the chuck itself. A poor chuck on a good spindle produces a poor result, and replacing a cheap chuck with a quality keyless one is the cheapest accuracy upgrade available.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check It With a Dial Indicator</h3>

          <p>
            Mount a ground rod in the chuck, set a dial indicator against it and rotate the spindle by hand. Under two thousandths is good for a woodworking machine, and under one is what a metalworking machine should achieve.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Drill Press Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0.002 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Acceptable runout for woodwork</p>
              </div>
              <div>
