        <!-- SECTION 2: Benchtop or Floor Standing -->
        <div id="benchtop-vs-floor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Benchtop or Floor Standing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Format is the first decision and it comes down to workpiece size and available space more than capability.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Swing is measured as twice the throat depth.</strong> A 12 inch drill press has six inches from the column to the spindle centre, so it can reach the middle of a twelve inch wide board. This is the number that limits what you can drill.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Floor machines take tall work.</strong> The column extends below the table, so a long workpiece can hang down beside it. Drilling into the end of a table leg is straightforward on a floor machine and impossible on a benchtop one.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Benchtop machines still occupy a bench.</strong> A heavy benchtop press is not being lifted off between jobs, so it consumes bench space permanently. The space saving over a floor machine is smaller than it first appears.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Mass matters more than format.</strong> A heavy benchtop machine bolted to a solid bench outperforms a light floor standing one. Weight damps vibration and resists deflection, which is what accuracy comes from.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Quill travel limits hole depth per plunge.</strong> Three inches or more is useful; two is restrictive, because deeper holes then require repositioning the table mid-hole, which is awkward and imprecise.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Table size and slots matter every day.</strong> A small table with narrow slots makes clamping awkward, and clamping is a safety requirement. Most owners of small-table machines end up building an auxiliary wooden table with a fence.
          </p>
        </div>

