        <!-- SECTION 4: Speed, Feed and Not Burning Bits -->
        <div id="speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Speed, Feed and Not Burning Bits
          </h2>

          <p>
            Running at the wrong speed is the most common drill press mistake and it destroys bits faster than anything else.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Larger bits need slower speeds.</strong> What matters is the speed at the cutting edge, which increases with diameter. A large forstner bit at high speed burns wood and dulls immediately, where the same speed suits a small twist bit.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Metal needs far slower speeds than wood.</strong> Steel generates heat that destroys the cutting edge, so metal drilling runs slow with steady pressure and cutting fluid. Running steel at wood speeds is how people conclude their bits are poor quality.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Steady pressure beats intermittent pressure.</strong> A bit rubbing rather than cutting generates heat and work hardens metal, making the hole harder to finish than when you started. Enough feed to produce a continuous chip is the target.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Peck drilling clears deep holes.</strong> Withdrawing periodically clears chips that would otherwise pack into the flutes, jam the bit and burn the hole. In deep holes this is the difference between a clean hole and a stuck bit.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use cutting fluid on metal.</strong> Even a light oil dramatically extends bit life and improves the finish, and in aluminium it prevents the material welding to the cutting edge. This is not optional for anything beyond thin sheet.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Change speed more often than feels convenient.</strong> The reason variable speed machines are worth paying for is not the range but the friction: a belt change takes minutes and gets skipped, whereas a dial gets used.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Big Bit, Slow Speed</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              The single rule that saves the most bits: as bit diameter goes up, spindle speed must come down. A three inch hole saw or a large forstner bit wants a few hundred rpm, not a few thousand. Running large cutters fast burns the wood, ruins the edge, and produces a hole with a scorched rim that no amount of sanding fully removes.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Setting Up and Tuning -->
        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Setting Up and Tuning
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most drill presses arrive needing an hour of attention, and machines that disappoint usually got none.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Square the table to the spindle.</strong> Put a bent rod in the chuck and rotate it to touch the table at front and back, then left and right. Any gap means the table is not perpendicular and every hole will be angled.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check and set belt tension.</strong> Too loose and the belt slips under load, which shows as a bit stalling in a cut. Too tight and it loads the bearings unnecessarily. There should be modest deflection at the midpoint of the span.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clean the spindle taper and chuck arbor.</strong> Any grease, swarf or protective coating between the taper and the arbor prevents a proper seat and introduces runout that has nothing to do with the machine. Clean both with solvent before assembly.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Set the quill return spring correctly.</strong> Too weak and the quill drifts down; too strong and it fights you and snaps back. Most machines allow adjustment and most arrive somewhere approximate.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Build an auxiliary table with a fence.</strong> A plywood table bolted to the cast table, with a sacrificial insert and a fence, transforms a drill press for woodworking. It supports work, prevents tear-out on the underside, and makes repeatable positioning easy.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Bolt light machines to the bench.</strong> A benchtop press that walks under load is both inaccurate and dangerous. Four bolts through the base into a solid bench solves the entire problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Chucks, Bits and Accessories -->
        <div id="accessories" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Chucks, Bits and Accessories
          </h2>

          <p>
            A drill press is a spindle over a table, and most of what makes it useful is what you attach to it and put underneath it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Upgrade the chuck before anything else.</strong> On budget machines the chuck is where the cost was saved, and since runout is cumulative through spindle, arbor and chuck, a poor one wastes a good spindle. A quality keyless chuck is the cheapest meaningful accuracy improvement available.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Brad point bits belong in wood.</strong> A twist bit wanders on entry because it has no centre point, whereas a brad point spur locates exactly on a mark and cuts a clean rim. For dowel holes and anything visible, the difference is obvious.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Forstner bits cut flat bottomed holes.</strong> Overlapping holes, angled entries and large clean recesses are all forstner territory, and none of them work with a twist bit. Run them slow and clear the chips often, because they generate a lot of heat.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A machine vice is worth owning.</strong> For metal work, a small drill press vice bolted to the table holds parts squarely and safely in a way clamps struggle to. It also makes repeat positioning far easier.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Sanding drums turn it into a shaping machine.</strong> With the work supported on an auxiliary table and a drum in the chuck, a drill press smooths curved edges acceptably. It burns a ring into the abrasive at one height unless the machine oscillates, so raise and lower the table periodically.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A sacrificial backing board prevents blowout.</strong> A hole drilled through into thin air tears the underside as the bit exits. A piece of scrap clamped underneath supports those fibres and produces a clean exit, which costs nothing and takes seconds.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Drill Press Safety -->
        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Drill Press Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Drill presses seem benign compared with saws and they cause a specific and severe category of injury.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clamp the workpiece, always.</strong> A bit that catches spins the workpiece into a blur, and holding it by hand means your hand is struck by the edge of a rotating object. This is the injury drill presses are known for, and it is entirely prevented by a clamp.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Sheet metal is the worst case.</strong> A twist bit breaking through thin metal grabs violently and spins the sheet like a blade. Metal must be clamped without exception, and a step bit or a slower feed at breakthrough reduces the grab.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Remove the chuck key before starting.</strong> A key left in the chuck becomes a projectile. Self ejecting keys exist and keyless chucks avoid the problem entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>No gloves, no loose sleeves, tie hair back.</strong> A rotating spindle catches fabric and pulls it in faster than you can react. Gloves in particular are dangerous around any rotating machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Eye protection every time.</strong> Metal swarf comes off hot and sharp, and a bit that snaps sends fragments in unpredictable directions. Safety glasses are the minimum.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Set the depth stop rather than judging by eye.</strong> Beyond producing consistent holes, a depth stop prevents drilling into the table itself, which damages the cast surface, ruins the bit, and on a through hole can leave the workpiece pinned to the table. Setting it takes seconds and removes a whole class of mistakes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Let the machine stop before reaching in.</strong> A spindle coasting after the switch is off is still turning fast enough to catch a sleeve or a finger. Wait for it to stop completely before clearing chips or changing anything.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Frequently Asked Questions -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best drill press?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                NOVA Voyager DVR if budget allows, combining the lowest runout we measured with electronic speed control that removes belt changes entirely. WEN 4214T offers variable speed at a fraction of the price, and JET JDP-17 is the pick for a heavy traditional floor machine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size drill press do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Swing is twice the throat depth, so a 12 inch press reaches the centre of a 12 inch wide board. Twelve inches covers most woodworking; larger workpieces or metalwork benefit from 15 to 17 inches. Also check quill travel, since under three inches limits hole depth per plunge.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is spindle runout and why does it matter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is wobble in the spindle, so the bit describes a small circle rather than spinning true. It makes holes oversized by twice the runout and causes small bits to wander and snap. Under two thousandths of an inch is good for woodworking, and under one for metalwork.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Benchtop or floor standing drill press?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Floor standing if you need to drill into the end of long workpieces, since the column lets them hang beside the machine. Benchtop otherwise, though a heavy benchtop press occupies bench space permanently anyway. Mass matters more than format for accuracy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What speed should I run a drill press at?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Slower as the bit gets larger, because cutting edge speed increases with diameter. Large forstner bits and hole saws want a few hundred rpm. Metal runs far slower than wood with cutting fluid and steady feed, and running steel at wood speeds is what ruins most bits.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to clamp work on a drill press?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, every time. A bit that catches spins the workpiece into a blur, and a hand holding it is struck by the edge of a rotating object. Thin sheet metal is the worst case because a twist bit grabs violently at breakthrough. This is the injury drill presses cause.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Drill Press Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Drill press problems fall into two groups: setup that was never done, and speeds that were never changed.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Holding the Workpiece by Hand</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                When a bit catches, the workpiece becomes a spinning blade and the hand holding it takes the impact. Thin sheet metal is the worst case because a twist bit grabs hard at breakthrough. A clamp or a machine vice costs very little and eliminates the injury this machine is known for.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Machine at One Speed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Large bits at high speed burn wood and destroy their own cutting edges, and metal at wood speeds does the same. On a belt drive machine changing speed takes a few minutes, which is exactly why it gets skipped. Make the change anyway, or buy a machine with a dial.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never Squaring the Table</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A table that is not perpendicular to the spindle angles every hole the machine drills, and most presses arrive close but not exact. Rotating a bent rod in the chuck to touch the table front, back, left and right reveals it in two minutes and it is adjustable.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the Original Cheap Chuck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Runout is cumulative through the spindle, arbor and chuck, so a poor chuck ruins a good spindle. On budget machines the chuck is where the cost was saved, and replacing it with a quality keyless chuck is the cheapest meaningful accuracy upgrade available.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

